AB  - Accurate and timely surface defect detection is essential to ensuring the quality and surface integrity of steel products, as it can save manufacturing costs and prevent product rejections later. This task is considerably more challenging in industrial contexts because to high dimensional feature spaces, substantial class imbalance in defect data, and missing values. This research introduces a comprehensive ensemble learning framework for predicting steel surface defects, primarily concentrating on defect-free galvannealed (GA) and galvanized steel surfaces utilized in the manufacturing of vehicle body panels. The proposed method enhances data quality and discriminative feature representation by integrating a novel Multi View Imputation and Feature Encoding (MVIFE) technique with feature importance ranking derived from the Maximal Information Coefficient (MIC). MVIFE uses Bayesian Ridge regression for iterative multivariate imputation, filtering out missing values by feature, and storing categorical features. This makes it a strong tool for managing inaccurate industrial data. To identify relevant parameters that exhibit both linear and nonlinear dependencies with the target variable, MIC scores are utilized. An ensemble classification architecture that uses Bagging, Boosting, Blending, and Max Voting operates on the altered feature set. A real-world dataset of steel defects from the Tata Steel Database, which is highly imbalanced, shows that the proposed approach is effective. We apply the Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) to address class imbalance and evaluate the model’s performance using accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. According to experimental data, the proposed ensemble framework consistently outperforms single learning models. Blending gets the best overall F1-Score, Max Voting gets higher recall, and Blending gets better accuracy. These results show that the suggested method is useful and reliable for accurately predicting defects on steel surfaces.
