AB  - Accurate prediction of the characteristic variables of dam outburst is crucial for the formulation of landslide dam failure contingency plans. To overcome the limitations of low prediction accuracy and computational efficiency of conventional methods due to the nonlinearity of landslide dam characteristic parameters, a novel landslide dam outburst characteristic variable forecasting model integrating long short-term memory networks (LSTM) and improved sparrow search algorithm (ISSA) algorithm was proposed. Firstly, several improvement strategies, including chaos initialization (CI), Cauchy mutation (CM), and opposition-based learning (OBL), are incorporated into the baseline sparrow search algorithm (SSA), and the ISSA is employed to optimize the hyperparameters of the LSTM. The resulting ISSA-LSTM hybrid regression model was validated using a synthetically generated dataset of dam outburst characteristic variables. Comparative models include standalone LSTM, LSTM tuned by other meta-heuristic algorithms, support vector regression (SVR), and back propagation neural network (BPNN). Results indicate that the ISSA-LSTM model, which effectively resists local optima and captures nonlinear relationships, surpasses other models in predictive capability. On the test set, it attains average values of root mean square error (RMSE), determination coefficient (R2), and Nash-Sutcliffe efficiency coefficient (NSE) of 827.81, 0.58, and 0.41, respectively, representing improvements of 32.03 %, 25.96 %, and 76.15 % over the baseline LSTM.
