AB  - Classifying chest radiographs is crucial for diagnosing respiratory disorders, enabling early detection and treatment. Existing image enhancement algorithms lack controlled contrast enhancement and fail to redistribute pixel intensities effectively, while traditional models struggle with imbalanced datasets, resulting in biased and less accurate outcomes, especially for minority classes. This study proposes a novel approach, ‘Histogram Clamped Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization enhanced Hybrid Weighted Average Stacking Ensemble Deep Neural Network’ (hcCLAHE-HWASDNN), for classifying chest radiographs into four categories: ‘pneumonia’, ‘normal’, ‘lung opacity’, and ‘Coronavirus Disease 2019’ (COVID-19). The ‘Histogram Clamped Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization’ (hcCLAHE) algorithm regulates contrast enhancement and redistributes excess pixel intensities for balanced processing. The ‘Hybrid Weighted Average Stacking’ (HWAS) method enhances robustness and accuracy by dynamically adjusting weights based on model performance, addressing imbalanced datasets and improving minority class predictions. The ensemble integrates Residual Network 18 layers (ResNet-18), Efficient Convolutional Neural Network Base Model 0 (EfficientNet-B0), and Alex Krizhevsky’s Neural Network (AlexNet), combined using a weighted average ensemble strategy for improved classification accuracy. To ensure interpretability, Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (GradCAM) highlights critical regions influencing predictions, while saliency mapping identifies high-impact areas in classification. These visualization techniques provide deeper insights into infectious regions of radiographs. Evaluated on a dataset of 53,986 chest X-rays, the proposed model achieves 97.85% accuracy, outperforming individual deep learning models and baseline methods. It demonstrates high sensitivity and specificity, particularly for pneumonia and COVID-19 cases, offering a robust solution for chest radiograph classification while overcoming limitations of conventional methods.
