AB  - Analyzing sentiment in social media image–text pairs presents substantial challenges, primarily due to the frequent presence of contradictory or ambiguous signals. Existing methods are constrained by two key limitations. Primarily, they analyze individual samples in isolation, thereby neglecting critical semantic relationships that are essential for accurate sentiment analysis. Furthermore, they exhibit insufficient resilience against the noise and inconsistencies that are intrinsic to multimodal data. To address these challenges, we propose a novel framework, the Noise-Aware Graph Network (NAGN). Specifically, NAGN introduces three key innovations: First, a Graph-Based Semantic Correlation module that constructs adaptive heterogeneous graphs to capture task-relevant relationships between samples, enabling refined feature extraction via structured message propagation. Second, a Noise-Aware Adaptive Fusion mechanism that assigns reliability scores to graph-enhanced features, guiding multi-head attention to prioritize informative signals and mitigate noise. Third, a Joint Multi-Objective Optimization strategy that integrates classification loss with supervised contrastive learning for robust cross-modal alignment. Extensive evaluations on three established multimodal sentiment benchmarks demonstrate that NAGN consistently outperforms existing methods. From an engineering application perspective, this work advances multimodal sentiment analysis by achieving superior performance and establishing a methodological foundation for context-driven relational understanding, moving beyond isolated instance processing. To facilitate reproducibility, the source code and trained models associated with this work are publicly available at https://github.com/AnJeray/NAGA.
