AB  - Electroencephalography (EEG) plays a vital role in psychiatric disorder analysis with Artificial Intelligence (AI) enabling applications such as automated diagnosis, emotion detection and mental state monitoring. However, the classification and interpretation of EEG data remain challenging due to noisy signals and high-dimensional features. The EEG Mental Health Disorder (MHD) dataset consists of 945 samples with 1149 features including EEG readings, demographic attributes and disorder labels across seven major psychiatric conditions where mood disorder (28.15 %) is the most prevalent. Traditional Transformer models are inefficient in handling such large, noisy datasets due to the fixed encoding and limited feature discrimination ability. To overcome these issues, the proposed Adaptive Threshold Dynamic Feature Ranking (ATDFR) approach performs robust feature selection by dynamically discarding non-informative EEG features. For improved representation learning, a LogNorm Embedding is applied in the Transformer by utilizing logarithmic normalization and the Momentum Enhanced Adam (ME-Adam) optimizer to convert tokens into more informative lower dimensional vectors. This combination forms the Momentum Enhanced LogNorm Transformer (MELT). Moreover, EEG analysis depends on dynamic interactions between frequency bands like Alpha, Beta and Gamma. To capture these complex dependencies, a Dependency Aware Multi-Head Attention (DMHA) mechanism is introduced which refines attention weights based on inter-feature relationships. Evaluation using the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, paired t-tests and p-values confirms the superior performance of the proposed model. The baseline LogNorm Transformer achieves 88.99 % accuracy and ME-Adam improves it to 92.54 %. Ultimately, MELT with DMHA achieves 96.96 % accuracy with enhanced convergence, stability and reduced overfitting in EEG based mental health classification.
