AB  - Seedling quality assessment is a critical task in forestry, traditionally relying on subjective expert judgment. This study introduces a lightweight and interpretable machine learning framework, termed IFLNBA (Interpretable Framework via Labels, Naive Bayes, and Ablation), which employs Gaussian Naive Bayes for binary classification of seedling quality under small-sample conditions. Expert-derived rules guide the labeling process, enabling effective model training on the limited FSLDSR (Forest Seedling dataset for Learning in Data-Scarce Regimes) dataset. The classifier achieves a test accuracy of 91.6% and a macro F1-score of 0.89. To enhance transparency, SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) and LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations) are applied for global and local interpretability. Feature ablation analysis identifies PlantHeight(The vertical height of the seedling from the ground level (at the root collar) to the shoot apex) as the most critical attribute, whose removal reduces accuracy by 11.1%, whereas removing GroundDiameter(a term used in forestry measurement, referring to the diameter of a seedling or tree at a specific height close to the ground) increases accuracy by 7.4%, indicating feature redundancy. Furthermore, model parameters are translated into human-readable rules, and a standalone prediction logic is reconstructed, achieving 100% consistency with the trained model. The proposed framework effectively balances accuracy, interpretability, and deployability, providing a reproducible solution for practical seedling selection and serving as a reference for transparent AI(Artificial Intelligence) applications in ecological decision-making.
