AB  - Federated Learning (FL), as a distributed machine learning strategy, enables collaborative model training through the transmission of updated model parameters. However, federated systems are frequently subject to malicious attacks that may lead to sensitive data leakage. To address this, Differential Privacy (DP) has been introduced to provide effective privacy protection, albeit often at the cost of reduced model accuracy. This typically results in frequent parameter exchanges and substantial communication overhead. In this paper, we propose a Federated Learning Algorithm with Differentially Private and Event-Triggered Communication (ETC-FedDP), which integrates differential privacy and event-triggered mechanism into a unified federated learning framework to simultaneously reduce communication cost and ensure privacy protection. We establish asymptotic convergence analysis for the proposed framework and conduct rigorous privacy budget analysis, providing formal proofs for both convergence guarantees and privacy preservation. Finally, the practicality of our algorithm is validated on three commonly used image classification datasets. Experimental results show that the test accuracy of our algorithm is comparable to that of the Federated Averaging algorithm and shows improvement compared to baseline algorithms.
