KW  - Boundary condition
AB  - To ensure the structural safety of double-lined water transfer tunnels in deeply buried soft strata, this study proposes a physics-informed neural network (PINN) approach. This approach integrates limited monitoring data with physical laws to infer the coupled stress state of inner and outer linings. First, the physics-informed neural network (PINN) is utilized to incorporate the principle of minimum potential energy and the four-parameter damage model into a deep learning framework to establish a physics-driven model of the tunnel structure. Subsequently, based on the Galerkin variational form and the energy minimization principle, a residual-form loss function integrating physical constraints and data-driven elements is designed, and the model parameters are optimized. Finally, the effectiveness of the proposed method in back-analyzing the magnitude of external loads is verified using a circular example with analytical solutions. The results indicate that the optimization using the residual-form loss function can accurately converge to the expected load values, thereby proving the feasibility of the method. At the same time, the method is used to invert the force state of the tunnel lining using the double-lined tunnel model as a numerical example. The influence of the number of monitoring points and the measurement accuracy on the inversion results is analyzed. Ultimately, the method was successfully validated using field monitoring data from an actual tunnel section, demonstrating its practical applicability and robustness. This analysis further verifies the applicability of the method in practical engineering. It also provides a scientific basis for the safety assessment of the tunnel structure.
