AB  - The forward modeling and parametric inversion of mechanical waves in physical systems are of significant importance in a multitude of scientific and engineering disciplines. Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs), which have the potential to seamlessly integrate physical models and observational data, have emerged as an effective tool for wavefield modeling and inversion. Nevertheless, when solving for wavefields with multi-frequency and high-frequency characteristics, the inherent spectral bias and loss convergence imbalance of PINNs ultimately result in their inability to accurately model wave propagation. This paper proposes a new framework, the Physics-informed Fourier neural network (PIFNN), to address these issues by incorporating Fourier feature mapping and adaptive loss weights based on the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) theory. The robustness and effectiveness of the PIFNN method are demonstrated by improved accuracy in simulating wave propagation in numerical experiment. Furthermore, the training dynamics of wave propagation simulated by PIFNN are analyzed through the lens of NTK to enhance interpretability.
