AB  - At unsignalized intersections, autonomous vehicle (AV) motion planning faces significant safety and performance challenges. The primary difficulty stems from observation uncertainties caused by occlusions and the behavior of other agents. To this end, we propose a motion planning approach for unsignalized intersections that explicitly handles observation uncertainty, comprising an observation uncertainty topology reasoning (OUTR) module and an observation uncertainty constrained proximal policy optimization (OUC-PPO) motion planning module. The OUTR models the observation uncertainty of the agent and the environment as node and edge topologies. It explicitly captures the agent’s uncertain future motion and environmental uncertainty due to occlusion, enabling better assessment of how observation uncertainty may affect downstream motion planning. In the OUC-PPO motion planning module, we developed a reinforcement learning (RL) based planning method that incorporates OUTR. This method features a dual safety constraint mechanism: the first is a feasible value function (FVF) learned offline, and the second introduces soft constraints via multimodal finite state traversal. This design effectively enhances the overall planning performance under observation uncertainty. Experimental results show that compared with the baseline method, the proposed method can execute a safer driving strategy at unsignalized intersections, and maintain higher driving efficiency and comfort.
