AB  - Accurate forecasting of fine particulate matter is essential for protecting public health and supporting air-quality management; however, existing models often struggle with nonlinear, nonstationary behavior, limited multiscale representation, and poor generalization across cities and pollutant types. Although numerous deep learning and statistical approaches have been proposed, current literature still lacks a unified framework capable of handling both multivariate pollutant-meteorology interactions and univariate fine particulate matter dynamics while maintaining strong predictive fidelity across diverse environmental conditions. To address these gaps, this study introduces a novel forecasting strategy that integrates Complete Ensemble Empirical Mode Decomposition with Adaptive Noise for multiscale signal decomposition, Sample Entropy for complexity-based clustering of intrinsic mode functions, and an Adaptive Channel Transformer optimized through evolutionary search to enhance temporal and channel-dependent learning. This combination provides a structured, noise-aware, and frequency-rich representation of air-quality signals, enabling robust prediction under both feature-based and rolling-window settings. Experiments across four major Chinese cities, Guangzhou, Shanghai, Shenzhen, and Beijing, demonstrate significant improvements over state-of-the-art benchmarks. In feature-based prediction, the approach achieves Coefficient of Determination of 0.987, 0.985, 0.987, and 0.993, and test Root Mean Square Error of 0.78, 2.20, 0.62, and 1.75, respectively. Strong performance is also observed for univariate forecasting and for additional pollutants, reflecting excellent temporal alignment between predicted and observed concentrations. Overall, the results show that combining Complete Ensemble Empirical Mode Decomposition with Adaptive Noise, Sample Entropy-based representation learning, and an optimized Adapformer yields a highly accurate and generalizable framework for comprehensive air-quality forecasting and decision support.
