AB  - This article presents a discrete error-orthogonalization gradient neural network (EOGNN) for solving time-varying linear equations (TVLEs) arising in engineering applications. Conventional numerical methods often incur tracking delays when coefficient matrices change over time, as these methods cannot efficiently follow rapidly varying dynamics. Existing gradient neural networks (GNNs) alleviate this issue through derivative-compensation schemes; however, their discrete implementations may suffer from increased computational overhead due to high-order numerical differentiation and matrix inversion. To address these limitations, an orthogonality-driven discrete solution framework is established. First, using a Taylor expansion, a first-order discretized GNN is recast as a second-order-accurate error-monitoring system. Based on this formulation, a fast-converging GNN for static linear equations, termed SGNN, is derived, which follows an orthogonal descent trajectory of the error. By introducing a time-delay term and a linear prediction mechanism for the orthogonal error direction, the orthogonal dynamics are extended to time-varying scenarios, yielding the EOGNN model. Numerical experiments on TVLEs with dimensions ranging from 10 to 200 show that EOGNN outperforms representative baselines. Trajectory tracking simulations and physical experiments of a redundant manipulator further confirm the practical effectiveness of the proposed approach.
