AB  - Accurate ripeness grading of multiple fruit species in uncontrolled orchard environments is crucial for automated harvesting. However, this task is hindered by a fundamental gap in existing datasets: benchmarks with high class diversity are typically captured in controlled laboratory settings, while those with scene realism are often limited to a single fruit species. This dichotomy impedes the development of generalizable models. To address this challenge, we introduce the Comprehensive and Diverse Fruit Ripeness Benchmark (CDFRB). Building on this benchmark, we propose MaturNet, a specialized detection model designed for fine-grained ripeness grading. The model utilizes a Spatial-Frequency Enhancement (SFE) module to capture critical surface textures, a Hierarchical Feature Aggregator (HFA) to enhance feature robustness, and a Cross-Scale Interaction Module (CSIM) to facilitate dynamic multi-scale fusion. Extensive experiments on the CDFRB benchmark demonstrate that MaturNet achieves an Average Precision (AP) of 62.6%, significantly outperforming existing mainstream detectors. Our work provides a solid foundational platform and a high-performance baseline model for research on large-scale fruit ripeness detection in real-world environments.
