AB  - This paper addresses alarm floods, which occur when many alarms are displayed on human–machine interface screens in a short period of time. Specifically, an information-theoretic approach is proposed, where each text-based alarm tag in an industrial control system is represented with a probability distribution, such that divergence can be characterized for displayed alarms belonging to different alarm floods, enabling effective similarity analysis. In addition, methods for dynamic clustering and early classification of alarm floods are also proposed, while considering uncertainty in the display of alarms, in terms of their relative display time and order as well as occurrence, hence addressing practical scenarios when alarm floods may evolve and change over time due to changes in operational conditions. The effectiveness of the proposed approach and methods are demonstrated using alarm floods generated in the Tennessee Eastman process.
