AB  - The timely detection of road cracks is essential for ensuring smooth and safe transportation. However, crack detection under zebra crossing interference has the problem of low accuracy and efficiency. In particular, the edge geometry information of crack imaging is complex: there are fluctuations in different directions and with different rules, which affect target feature capture and perception reconstruction. Crack edge information is lost. To address these issues, this paper proposes a deep learning model--Feature Adaptive Extraction and Gated Fusion Network (FAGFNet) for road crack segmentation. The model incorporates three key modules: (1) a Multiscale Feature Space Coupling (MFSC) is proposed to obtain features of cracks at various scales; (2) an innovative Feature Adaptive Analysis (FAA) is designed to enhance the edge geometry information capture capability of crack images; (3) A Contextual Gating Feature Attention (CGFA) is used to filter redundant features and finely fuse contextual information. Under zebra crossing interference and image edge geometry information, the complex crack segmentation accuracy is improved by the above three modules. We evaluated FAGFNet on its crack recognition capability for both asphalt and concrete pavements. Compared with 11 state-of-the-art deep learning models, the proposed FAGFNet demonstrates superior performance across various complex scenarios. The proposed model achieves the Intersection over Union (IoU) of 89.57 % and F1-score of 92.17 % for crack segmentation. In addition, the segmentation efficiency of FAGFNet reaches 21.84 Frames Per Second (FPS), which can realize real-time segmentation. The above experimental results show that the proposed FAGFNet model has better robustness and generalization ability in the field of road crack segmentation. The code for pavement crack segmentation are publicly available at https://github.com/gwt-ZJU/FAGFNet-.
