AB  - This study develops an ensemble machine learning (ML) framework to predict non-spectral ground motion (GM) intensity measures (IMs)—significant-duration parameters, Cumulative Absolute Velocity (CAV), Peak Ground Velocity (PGV), and Housner Intensity (SI)—using a regional Turkish GM dataset (747 earthquakes, 23,147 records). Data selection was based on explicit seismological criteria (moment magnitude (Mw)≥3.5, hypocentral depth <30 km (km), epicentral distance <200 km, and ≥5 station recordings per event) to ensure regional consistency. Model development was performed using a five-fold cross-validation scheme, where performance was rigorously evaluated on independent test folds across four Mw bins (3.5≤ Mw ≤ 4.5, 4.5<Mw ≤ 5.5, 5.5<Mw ≤ 6.5, Mw > 6.5) and the full dataset to capture potential nonlinear scaling effects and ensure generalisability across distinct seismic energy levels. Four ensemble ML algorithms—Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Random Forest (RF), Gradient Boosting Regressor (GBR), and Stacking-GBR—were employed. The stacking-GBR exhibits higher predictive capabilities across all IMs and magnitude bins, achieving coefficient of determination (R2) values up to 0.99 and maintaining high accuracy for Mw > 6.5, despite data scarcity. Model explainability via SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) shows that magnitude and distance metrics dominate velocity/energy-based IMs, whereas source-to-site geometry and path parameters govern duration-based IMs. Residual analysis indicate stable, low-bias behaviour for PGV and CAV across bins, while greater dispersion in duration at small and large events, reflected epistemic uncertainty from data imbalance. The results support integrating explainable ensemble ML models, particularly Stacking-GBR, into seismic-hazard assessment and performance-based earthquake-engineering workflows, emphasizing the need for more large-magnitude data to stabilize duration predictions.
